#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfc6b856-4f58-5f40-bbc1-d8c18ae5096a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365202} {:answer/id #uuid "767723fb-d32d-5a40-8828-30177dc2a0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365203} {:answer/id #uuid "e019e31f-ebc1-5bab-a719-499a4ea439c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365204} {:answer/id #uuid "16a59d8a-69e3-52c5-aa62-d8170533b658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365205} {:answer/id #uuid "befa8d07-e937-5866-bf06-12a6368ff3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for nonlinear systems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e019e31f-ebc1-5bab-a719-499a4ea439c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365204} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f82aee8-df8d-5697-a473-6e7ab9e13456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "638a5026-be36-5dd1-908b-d2c6d418484f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333365" :question/problem "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to slowly spiral towards the equilibrium. How should the equilibrium be classified?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Asymptotically stable.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e970316-5a61-5305-b09d-9d723ec78dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions approach the equilibrium only if the system is linear and all eigenvalues are real." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365209} {:answer/id #uuid "f07062c7-4321-5bd2-b573-99e7bf77edab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually leave a fixed neighborhood of it." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365210} {:answer/id #uuid "00583e6a-7009-5d81-8088-2b171ea09df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365211} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b0c803-30b8-5d6e-bb2a-ba067c853c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions that start close to the equilibrium remain close to it for all future times, but may not converge to it." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365212} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c8ae98-0576-5bd5-b062-7e93d61fdb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Solutions that start anywhere in the state space eventually remain in some fixed neighborhood of the equilibrium." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f07062c7-4321-5bd2-b573-99e7bf77edab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually leave a fixed neighborhood of it." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365210} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae10e357-6c37-5be1-a5f0-4f24aee3d8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "894667d1-f4aa-5975-a79e-d7ee80410cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335454" :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **unstable** equilibrium point?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSome solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually leave a fixed neighborhood of it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d51b72ac-fe84-5a85-bf9f-6590ba36101a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365216} {:answer/id #uuid "563df9ac-59dd-5948-b33a-7f2f45698aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365217} {:answer/id #uuid "bc80bd70-6373-5405-8ea2-0c48efcbb431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "exactly at" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d51b72ac-fe84-5a85-bf9f-6590ba36101a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365216} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce73fa4-cd6e-535b-8589-9486aed9240c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "936c31f3-b47d-53c8-8e41-1867a0425c0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "approach and move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365220} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa533f5-55dd-5183-aa53-7fb1e1f3fdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365221} {:answer/id #uuid "24cbf440-5702-5b14-be23-1d9807de0f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaa533f5-55dd-5183-aa53-7fb1e1f3fdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365221} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eba7bbc-7426-56e2-a152-0b1d0f3edbd1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaf5eecc-182b-535b-8acd-0e432c31f5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "may not converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365224} {:answer/id #uuid "4acf0ee1-86d8-5339-ad3d-9a791b184a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365225} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab58ee0-e242-5617-b1e8-3dadfb4074be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "never converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4acf0ee1-86d8-5339-ad3d-9a791b184a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365225} :answer-field/id #uuid "a087bdc5-b887-593b-bbbe-6bd5d35764d2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ab9c96d-932f-5272-8662-18e0a52dc3ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-335463" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point.The solutions that start sufficiently\n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-2}}\nit for all future time, and\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{remain close to}$ it for all future time, and $\\text{always converge to the equilibrium as }t\\to \\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee5e9183-0aad-523c-af69-eee680ddb6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365229} {:answer/id #uuid "c44ef9cc-e4bd-56e2-9433-6e1702a0ce90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365230} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5ef72d-9708-517b-bf8e-9dcd70a3051d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365231} {:answer/id #uuid "d985bc45-5ffa-5643-938e-e6a3042768e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365232} {:answer/id #uuid "39a692a8-9a4d-5345-8ca5-cb9e511c8722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd5ef72d-9708-517b-bf8e-9dcd70a3051d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365231} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c00a6fb-3f0f-508f-8224-56362ff2eb1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21ab42b7-8f9f-5826-a8e7-e56da9141cf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335647" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2y-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2(-x-2)-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\text{ }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-2=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=-(-2)-2=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9044c8e-6e38-522d-a2b9-c354d0c5bce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365236} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a24f4d-d822-5955-baa7-94529b97c46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365237} {:answer/id #uuid "fe291353-8421-5644-8181-844de499d13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365238} {:answer/id #uuid "0fdcaec4-5b59-52fe-903c-c9cde246ac03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365239} {:answer/id #uuid "bddd13b3-b9d6-54eb-9b5b-6a9d946b9a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bddd13b3-b9d6-54eb-9b5b-6a9d946b9a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365240} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff634de-92d2-5cbb-baca-e2cdb5489fd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aaeddc8-6572-55ca-bb11-3efda949f182" :question/math-academy-id "q-335657" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{3}(t)+x(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+x-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+(1-y)-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $x:$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=1-0=1.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get $x=1-1=0.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=1-(-1)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
